<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/semna"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/semna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Semna</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Semna was a group of Egyptian fortresses at the southern end of the Second Cataract of the Nile in Nubia, in present-day Sudan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/semna-dispatches"><span class="enc-name">Semna Dispatches</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Semna Dispatches are copies of reports sent from Egyptian fortresses in Nubia, such as Semna, to the administration at Thebes in the reign of Amenemhat III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/semnones"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/semnones.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Semnones</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Semnones were a Germanic people living between the Elbe and the Oder in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/semois"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/semois.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Semois</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Semois is a river of the Ardennes, about 210 kilometres long, rising near Arlon in the Belgian province of Luxembourg and winding west through deep meanders past…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sempach-charter"><span class="enc-name">Sempach Charter</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sempach Charter was an agreement of 1393 among the eight Swiss Confederate cantons and Solothurn setting common rules for conduct in war.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/semu"><span class="enc-name">Semu</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Semu was a category in the social hierarchy of the Yuan Dynasty, covering peoples of Central and Western Asian origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/semyon-dezhnev"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/semyon-dezhnev.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Semyon Dezhnev</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Semyon Dezhnev was a Russian cossack explorer of Siberia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/semyon-mikhailovich-of-novosil"><span class="enc-name">Semyon Mikhailovich of Novosil</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Semyon Mikhailovich was a Rurikid prince, a son of Mikhail of Chernigov, who was killed at the camp of Batu Khan in 1246.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sen-no-rikyu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sen no Rikyū</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sen no Rikyū was a Japanese tea master from a merchant family of Sakai who shaped the tea ceremony into its classic form.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sena-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sena-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sena dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sena dynasty ruled Bengal from the late 11th century to the early 13th century, after taking over from the Pala Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sena-gallica"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sena-gallica.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sena Gallica</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sena Gallica, modern Senigallia, is a town on the Adriatic coast of Italy, in the Marche.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sena-mozambique"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sena-mozambique.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sena, Mozambique</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sena is a town on the south bank of the Zambezi in central Mozambique.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senasammata-vikramabahu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senasammata-vikramabahu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senasammata Vikramabahu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senasammata Vikramabahu was the first king of Kandy, ruling from about 1469 to 1511.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senat-conservateur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senat-conservateur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sénat conservateur</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sénat conservateur was the senate of France under the Consulate and the First Empire, created by the constitution of 1799.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senate-of-forty-eight"><span class="enc-name">Senate of Forty-Eight</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senate of Forty-Eight was a council created in Florence in 1532, when the republic was abolished and Alessandro de' Medici became hereditary duke.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senate-of-milan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senate-of-milan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senate of Milan</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senate of Milan was the supreme court and highest council of the Duchy of Milan, created by Louis XII of France in 1499 after his conquest of the duchy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senchus-fer-n-alban"><span class="enc-name">Senchus fer n-Alban</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senchus fer n-Alban ('history of the men of Scotland') is an early medieval Irish text describing the kingdom of Dál Riata in western Scotland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sendai"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sendai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sendai</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sendai is the capital of Miyagi Prefecture and the largest city of the Tōhoku region in north-eastern Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sendai-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sendai-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sendai Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sendai Castle, also called Aoba Castle, was built from 1601 by Date Masamune on Mount Aoba above the city of Sendai in north-eastern Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senebkay"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senebkay.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senebkay</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senebkay was an Egyptian king of the Second Intermediate Period, probably of the Abydos Dynasty, who reigned around 1650 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/seneca-falls-convention.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Seneca Falls Convention</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Seneca Falls Convention was the first women's rights convention, held in the Wesleyan Chapel at Seneca Falls, New York, on 19–20 July 1848.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/seneca-lake"><span class="enc-name">Seneca Lake</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Seneca Lake is the largest of the Finger Lakes of western New York, about 60 km long and over 180 m deep.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senegal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senegal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senegal</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Senegal is a region of westernmost Africa, named after the Senegal River and now an independent country.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senegal-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senegal River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senegal River flows from the Guinea highlands to the Atlantic, forming much of the border between modern Senegal and Mauritania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senegambia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senegambia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senegambia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Senegambia is the region of West Africa between the Senegal and Gambia rivers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senekerim-hovhannes-artsruni"><span class="enc-name">Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni was the last king of Vaspurakan, the Armenian kingdom around Lake Van, ruling from 1003.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/seneslau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/seneslau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Seneslau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Seneslau was a Romanian voivode of the mid-13th century, known only from King Béla IV of Hungary's charter of 1247 to the Knights Hospitaller.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sengge-dzungar-ruler"><span class="enc-name">Sengge (Dzungar ruler)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sengge was ruler of the Dzungars from 1653 to 1670, the son and successor of Erdeni Batur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sengge-namgyal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sengge-namgyal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sengge Namgyal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sengge Namgyal was king of Ladakh in the western Himalayas from about 1616 to 1642, the greatest ruler of the Namgyal dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sengoku-period.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sengoku period</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sengoku period, or Warring States period, was an era of almost continuous civil war in Japan from the late fifteenth to the late sixteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senguttuvan"><span class="enc-name">Senguttuvan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senguttuvan was a Chera king of the Sangam age in southern India, praised in the fifth decade of the Patirruppattu for his campaigns.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senhime"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senhime.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senhime</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senhime was a granddaughter of Tokugawa Ieyasu and the eldest daughter of the second shogun, Tokugawa Hidetada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senlis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senlis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senlis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Senlis is a town in the Oise department of northern France, north of Paris.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sennacherib.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sennacherib</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sennacherib was king of the Neo-Assyrian Empire from 705 to 681 BCE, known for rebuilding Nineveh as a magnificent capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sennacheribs-destruction-of-babylon"><span class="enc-name">Sennacherib's destruction of Babylon</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 689 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">Sennacherib's destruction of Babylon took place in 689 BCE, when the Assyrian king captured the city after repeated rebellions and had it devastated.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senne"><span class="enc-name">Senne</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senne (Dutch Zenne) is a small river of central Belgium, about 100 km long, flowing north through Brussels to join the Dijle near Mechelen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sens"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sens.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sens</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sens is a town on the Yonne in Burgundy, France, known in Roman times as Agedincum, the chief town of the Senones.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sentence-of-guadalupe"><span class="enc-name">Sentence of Guadalupe</span><span class="enc-type">Peace · 1486</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sentence of Guadalupe was an arbitral ruling issued by Ferdinand II of Aragon at the monastery of Guadalupe on 21 April 1486.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senufo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senufo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senufo</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senufo are a people of West Africa living in northern Côte d'Ivoire, southern Mali and western Burkina Faso, speaking Gur languages.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senusret-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senusret I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senusret I was the second king of the Twelfth Dynasty of Egypt, son of Amenemhat I, reigning about 45 years in the twentieth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senusret-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senusret-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senusret II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senusret II was a king of the Twelfth Dynasty of Egypt in the nineteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/senusret-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Senusret III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senusret III was a pharaoh of the Twelfth Dynasty of Middle Kingdom Egypt, reigning in the 19th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senussi-order"><span class="enc-name">Senussi order</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Senussi order (Sanusiyya) was a Sufi religious and political brotherhood founded in the nineteenth century and centred in Cyrenaica in Libya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senzangakhona"><span class="enc-name">Senzangakhona</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Senzangakhona was chief of the small Zulu chiefdom in what is now KwaZulu-Natal in the late 18th and early 19th centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/senzeni-na"><span class="enc-name">Senzeni Na?</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">&quot;Senzeni Na?&quot; (&quot;What Have We Done?&quot;), also spelled Senzenina, is a South African folk song of the struggle against apartheid, sung in Xhosa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/seo-hui"><span class="enc-name">Seo Hui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Seo Hui was a Korean statesman and diplomat of the Goryeo period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/seokguram"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/seokguram.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Seokguram</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Seokguram is an artificial cave shrine on Mount Toham near Gyeongju, South Korea, built in the mid-8th century under the Unified Silla minister Kim Daeseong.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encycloped